(A) the twin with person P* (feature case)
  200 random worlds: data laws equal (exact) in 200; with |Sigma| >= 2 the kernels differ at every input in 130 of the 130 worlds with |Sigma| >= 2; with |Sigma| = 1 the twin is the person itself (asserted).

(B) N worlds with one data law: sum_i Pr_i[copy of person i] <= 1 for every procedure
  5 worlds share the data law (x uniform, y = T(x)) and have pairwise different persons
  n = 1: all 2401 deterministic procedures: max sum_i Pr_i[copy_i] = 1
  n = 2: 3000 random deterministic procedures: max sum = 1 (<= 1)
  n = 3: 3000 random deterministic procedures: max sum = 57/64 (<= 1)
  (a randomised procedure is a mixture of deterministic ones, so its sum is a mixture of these sums)

(C) ties in rho
  fair coin, h = b vs h = 1-b: kernels equal: True; data laws equal: False
  so 'equal kernels give equal data laws' fails with ties; the draft's wording after the proposition (same response function and coin law) is the correct one.

(D) latent-cause channel: is P* the kernel of some person (a fixed coin law, injective response)?
  200 random latent-cause worlds: P* has input-dependent mass profiles (so no person has kernel P*) in 200
